  "live_records": {
    "q-349471": {
      "dom_id": "step-q349471",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨(t-1)({t}^{2}+2),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8-{t}^{2})⟩,$ what is ${r}^{′}(-1)?$\n\n${r}^{′}(-1)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=(t-1)({t}^{2}+2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =(1)({t}^{2}+2)+(t-1)(2t) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+2)+2t(t-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(-1)=({(-1)}^{2}+2)+2(-1)((-1)-1)=7.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8-{t}^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{2t}{8-{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{8-{(-1)}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(-1) & =⟨{x}^{′}(-1),\\,{y}^{′}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,\\,\\frac{2}{7}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
