    "q-350219": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350219",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨4{e}^{2(t-1)},\\,-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)⟩,$ what is $|{r}^{′}(1)|?$\n\n$|{r}^{′}(1)|=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=4{e}^{2(t-1)},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=8{e}^{2(t-1)},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=8{e}^{0}=8.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1),$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1),\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =⟨{x}^{′}(1),\\,{y}^{′}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{r}^{′}(1)| & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{(-6)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
