    "q-349497": {
      "dom_id": "step-q349497",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}⟩,$ what is $|{r}^{′}(2)|?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$|{r}^{′}(2)|=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{12}{t},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(2)=\\frac{12}{2}=6.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=5t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(2)=5(2)=10.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2) & =⟨{x}^{′}(2),\\,{y}^{′}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{r}^{′}(2)| & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{136} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
