    "q-130407": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130407",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Find the derivative of  $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+3t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\end{aligned}]$ at $t=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+3t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2t+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(1)$ we substitute $t=1$ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2(1)+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{1} \\\\ \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\pi \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
