    "q-75032": {
      "dom_id": "step-q75032",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)⟩,$ ${r}^{″}(t)=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)={t}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
