    "q-75066": {
      "dom_id": "step-q75066",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{t-1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩,$ ${r}^{″}(t)=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=\\frac{t}{t-1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{(t-1)-t}{(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(t-1)^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{2}{(t-1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{(t-1)^{3}},\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
