    "q-347965": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347965",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Given that $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7\\end{aligned}],$ what is ${f}^{″}(0)?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{″}(0)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}^{″}(0)$ by substituting $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ -10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0+1 \\\\ -10(1) \\\\ -2(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
